rebrowser-bot-detector — vendored, self-hosted, OFFLINE build
=============================================================

Upstream: https://github.com/rebrowser/rebrowser-bot-detector  (MIT, see ./LICENSE)
Vendored files: index.js, index.css (the panel), plus rebrowser.html (this repo's host page,
one directory up), and UPSTREAM-README.md (the upstream README, unmodified, for reference).

This benchmark runs the panel fully offline on localhost and must NEVER contact a remote
host. The upstream index.js makes two network calls and the upstream page allowed remote
hosts in its CSP. The following MINIMAL, DOCUMENTED offline patches were applied to index.js
(each marked inline with `[stealthbench offline patch]`) to honour the self-host-only invariant
while preserving the panel's detection semantics:

  1. testCsp() — the upstream CSP-bypass probe loads a REMOTE <script src=...w3schools.com...>.
     Re-pointed at a local nonexistent path (./rebrowser/csp-probe-should-404.js): it 404s →
     onerror fires (exactly as a normal CSP-respecting browser behaves) → reports "CSP enabled"
     (rating -1), with zero network egress.

  2. testUseragent() — the upstream test fetch()es the latest Chrome release from a REMOTE
     endpoint (chromiumdash.appspot.com) to flag abnormal versions. The remote fetch is
     disabled (throws locally) so control falls through to the existing catch → neutral rating 0.
     The remote version comparison is not part of this benchmark's signal.

  3. initTests() — added a `window.__REBROWSER_DONE__` readiness flag (set ~1200ms after init)
     so the panel can be measured deterministically by an unattended driver.

The host page (../rebrowser.html) additionally tightens the Content-Security-Policy meta to a
self-only policy (no remote hosts), replacing the upstream policy that allowed cloudflareinsights.

No detection logic was otherwise altered. Inert attribution links (`<a href target="_blank">`)
inside result-note strings are retained but are never auto-loaded.
